      "source": [
        "##### 11. What is the difference between a set of bagged decision trees and a random forest? Be specific and precise!\n",
        "\n",
        "• Bagged Decision Trees: Each tree considers all features (columns) at every split. If one feature is very dominant, most trees will use it, making them look very similar (correlated).\n",
        "\n",
        "• Random Forest: Each tree can only look at a random subset of features at every split. This forces the trees to be completely different (decorrelated), which helps the model find hidden patterns and perform better.\n",
        "\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "MdzqHEy78xob"
      },
      "source": [
        "##### 12. Why might a random forest be superior to a set of bagged decision trees?\n",
        "> Hint: Consider the bias-variance tradeoff.\n",
        "\n",
        "A random forest is superior because it reduces variance (overfitting) better than bagging, while keeping bias low.\n",
        "\n",
        "• Bagging: Since all trees can see all columns, they often pick the same strong column and look exactly alike. When trees are identical, averaging their answers doesn't eliminate much error.\n",
        "\n",
        "• Random Forest: By forcing trees to pick from a random mix of columns, it makes the trees completely different from each other.\n",
        "In the bias-variance tradeoff, a random forest keeps the low bias of deep trees, but dramatically lowers the variance because averaging a group of diverse, different trees cancels out much more random noise and mistakes."
      ]
    },
